              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "jessicalynn13"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I made this for dinner for my southern husband and he LOVES it! If he hadn't been home when I made it he would've been convinced it took me all day. It comes close to real Jambalaya, and is a great substitute when time is an issue."
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Tom Long"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Cut recipe in half. Substituted Slap Ya Mama Cajun blend for seasonings and added a small amount of leftover chicken. Other good additions would be celery and green onions. Quick and easy skillet dinner.",
            "datePublished": "2023-12-05T11:17:14.309Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Leonidas Bigalow IV"
            },
            "reviewBody": "This is really good, really easy jambalaya.\n\nMy only criticism is, I think they're having you use WAY too much cooked rice. I would suggest adding in one cup at a time and then stopping when it looks like the desired consistency. I think if you just dumped in all the rice they suggest at once, it's going to be way too much rice.\n\nA couple of other minor tweaks:\n\n- Use a lot more hot sauce than they suggest. I would recommend Frank's Red Hot because it imparts a lot of flavor without too much heat.\n\n- You can pump up the flavor a little more by adding about a cup of chicken broth to the final product.",
            "datePublished": "2020-04-05T03:04:24.593Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "FizzyRibs1345"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Great for the extra rice I had in the freezer from Chinese food. Added turkey sausage and cooked shrimp (at the very end). I used stewed tomatoes (roughly chopped) for a little different flavor profile. Tony Chachere's Creole seasoning used freely, dash cayenne pepper, red pepper flakes and a little gochujang (because I was out of hot sauce). Able to use up quite a bit from the freezer.",
            "datePublished": "2024-11-17T01:15:46.208Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "3"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Alita-Geri Carter"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I liked this recipe as it does take about 1/3 of the time that it takes for me to make the normal Jambalaya that I make but I would add worcestershire sauce, dried bay leaves, parsely, cayenne pepper, oregano, cajun seasoning, and basil to this recipe. I also seasoned the meat and veggies while they were cooking so that the flavor would cook into the meat and veggies also. The original recipe does save quite a bit of time after the meal prep is done but it turns out to be bland if you don't add additional seasonings. Not bad at all for attempting to save time.",
            "datePublished": "2016-12-16T03:18:57.480Z"
          },
          {
